(function(){(()=>{"use strict";const e=(a,n,i,r,o,s,t)=>({g:a,p:n,n:i,f:r,how:o,ex:s,...t?{fig:t}:{}});window.PhysicaRankMagJee={chapter:"Magnetic Effects of Current and Magnetism",secs:["Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents","Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid","Force on moving charges and on wires","Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector","Torque on a loop and the galvanometer","Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole","Earth\u2019s magnetism","Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law"],table:[["Magnetic field B (tesla, T = N/(A m))","MT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9"],["Magnetic flux \u03A6 (weber, Wb = T m\xB2)","ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9"],["Permeability of free space \u03BC\u2080","MLT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB2 (T m/A)"],["Magnetic moment m = NIA","L\xB2A (A m\xB2)"],["Magnetic intensity H","L\u207B\xB9A (A/m)"],["Magnetisation M","L\u207B\xB9A (A/m)"],["Pole strength","LA (A m)"],["Susceptibility \u03C7 and relative permeability \u03BCr","dimensionless"],["Force per length between parallel wires","MT\u207B\xB2 (N/m)"],["Torsion constant k of a galvanometer","ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2 (N m/rad)"],["Gyromagnetic ratio e/2m","M\u207B\xB9TA (C/kg)"],["Torque on a dipole mB sin\u03B8","ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2 (N m)"]],formulas:[e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",1,"Biot\u2013Savart law","dB = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{I dl sin\u03B8;r\xB2} (vector form dB = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{I dl \xD7 r\u0302;r\xB2}) \xB7 \u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0 = 10\u207B\u2077 T m/A \xB7 \u03B8 is the angle between dl and the line from the element to the point \xB7 B = 0 at a point on the line of the current element \xB7 moving point charge: B = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{qv sin\u03B8;r\xB2}","Divide the conductor into elements, use the symmetry to see which components cancel, and add the rest. The direction comes from the right-hand rule: thumb along the current, fingers curl along B.","A proton moves at 10\u2076 m/s. At 1 mm from it, on a line perpendicular to its velocity, B = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 10\u2076 \xF7 10\u207B\u2076 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 T."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",1,"Long straight wire","infinite wire: B = {\u03BC\u2080I;2\u03C0d} = {2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I;d} \xB7 B \u221D {1;d} \xB7 lines are circles around the wire \xB7 semi-infinite wire, point on the perpendicular at its end: B = {\u03BC\u2080I;4\u03C0d} \xB7 point on the line of the wire: B = 0","Use B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I/d with d in metre. A wire that stops at the foot of the perpendicular gives exactly half the infinite value.","I = 10 A, d = 5 cm: B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xF7 0.05 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.","mgwire"),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",1,"Finite straight wire","B = {\u03BC\u2080I;4\u03C0d}(sin\u03B1 + sin\u03B2) \xB7 \u03B1 and \u03B2 are the angles the lines from the point to the two ends make with the perpendicular from the point \xB7 point on the bisector of a wire of length 2L: B = {\u03BC\u2080I;4\u03C0d}\xB7{2L;\u221A(L\xB2 + d\xB2)} \xB7 infinite wire: \u03B1 = \u03B2 = 90\xB0 gives {\u03BC\u2080I;2\u03C0d}","Draw the perpendicular from the point to the wire. Measure both angles from this perpendicular, then add the two sines. If the point is beyond an end, subtract.","Wire of length 20 cm, I = 10 A, point on the bisector at 10 cm: \u03B1 = \u03B2 = 45\xB0, B = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xF7 0.1 \xD7 2 \xD7 0.707 = 1.41 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",1,"Centre of a circular loop and of an arc","full loop of N turns: B = {\u03BC\u2080NI;2R} \xB7 arc of angle \u03B8 (in radian): B = {\u03BC\u2080I\u03B8;4\u03C0R} \xB7 semicircle: {\u03BC\u2080I;4R} \xB7 quarter circle: {\u03BC\u2080I;8R} \xB7 straight pieces whose line passes through the centre add nothing \xB7 direction by the right-hand rule on the current","Count the arcs as a fraction of the full circle: B = (\u03B8/2\u03C0) \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/2R. Add the arcs as vectors with their directions.","Semicircle of R = 20 cm, I = 5 A: B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5 \xF7 (4 \xD7 0.2) = 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T. A 90\xB0 arc with R = 10 cm and I = 4 A gives 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",1,"Axis of a circular loop","B = {\u03BC\u2080NIR\xB2;2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)} \xB7 centre (x = 0): {\u03BC\u2080NI;2R} \xB7 far away (x \u226B R): B = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{2m;x\xB3} with m = NIA \xB7 maximum value is at the centre \xB7 direction along the axis (right-hand rule)","Put x and R in the same unit and keep the 3/2 power. For x \u226B R the loop behaves like a bar magnet of moment NIA.","R = 3 cm, x = 4 cm, N = 1, I = 5 A: B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xF7 (2 \xD7 (25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)^(3/2)) = 2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T (the value at the centre is 1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T)."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",2,"Square and polygon loops","regular n-sided loop, distance d from the centre to a side: B = {n\u03BC\u2080I;2\u03C0d} sin{\u03C0;n} \xB7 square of side a: B = {2\u221A2 \u03BC\u2080I;\u03C0a} \xB7 n very large: B \u2192 {\u03BC\u2080I;2d}, the circle \xB7 each side of a polygon is a finite wire seen at angle \u03C0/n from the centre","Find the field of one side with the finite-wire formula (both angles \u03C0/n), then multiply by the number of sides.","Square of side 20 cm, I = 5 A: B = 2\u221A2 \xD7 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5 \xF7 (\u03C0 \xD7 0.2) = 2.83 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",2,"Rotating charged ring or disc","a ring of charge Q turning with \u03C9 is a current I = {Q\u03C9;2\u03C0} \xB7 centre: B = {\u03BC\u2080Q\u03C9;4\u03C0R} \xB7 disc of charge Q and radius R: B at the centre = {\u03BC\u2080Q\u03C9;2\u03C0R} \xB7 magnetic moment of the ring m = {1;2}Q\u03C9R\xB2","Turn the revolving charge into a current I = Q/T and use the loop formulas. For a disc add thin rings.","Ring of R = 10 cm, Q = 3 \u03BCC, \u03C9 = 100 rad/s: B = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 100 \xF7 0.1 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 T."),e("Biot\u2013Savart law and fields of simple currents",2,"Two fields at one point","fields at one point add as vectors \xB7 two perpendicular fields: B = \u221A(B\u2081\xB2 + B\u2082\xB2), at angle tan \u03B8 = {B\u2082;B\u2081} to the first \xB7 two parallel wires with currents in the same direction: the field is zero on the line midway between them \xB7 with opposite currents the fields add midway","Find the direction of each field with the right-hand rule first. Add magnitudes only when the directions are the same or opposite.","Two long wires 20 cm apart carry 10 A in opposite directions. Midway the fields add: 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xF7 0.1 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. With equal currents in the same direction the field midway is zero."),e("Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",1,"Ampere\u2019s circuital law","\u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080I_enc \xB7 I_enc is the current that passes through the loop, taken with signs \xB7 useful when the field has symmetry (long wire, cylinder, solenoid, toroid, sheet) \xB7 currents outside the path do not enter I_enc but still make the field","Choose a closed path on which B is constant (or zero) along each piece, write B \xD7 length = \u03BC\u2080I_enc, and solve for B.","A path that encloses 5 A one way and 2 A the other way has \u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 3 = 3.77 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T m."),e("Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",1,"Cylindrical conductor","outside (r \u2265 a): B = {\u03BC\u2080I;2\u03C0r} \xB7 inside a solid cylinder with uniform current: B = {\u03BC\u2080Ir;2\u03C0a\xB2}, growing linearly from the axis \xB7 at the surface the two formulas agree \xB7 a hollow tube: B = 0 inside \xB7 a thin sheet of current density K: B = {\u03BC\u2080K;2} on each side","For a solid cylinder only the current inside the radius r counts: I_enc = I r\xB2/a\xB2. Outside, treat it as a wire on the axis.","I = 10 A, a = 2 cm, r = 1 cm: B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.01 \xF7 (0.02)\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, which is half the value at the surface."),e("Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",1,"Solenoid","inside a long solenoid: B = \u03BC\u2080nI = {\u03BC\u2080NI;L} \xB7 uniform and along the axis \xB7 at an end (on the axis): B = {\u03BC\u2080nI;2} \xB7 outside a long solenoid B \u2248 0 \xB7 B does not depend on the radius or the position inside \xB7 with an iron core: B = \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr nI","Use n = turns per metre (N/L). Convert turns per cm to turns per metre first (\xD7100). The field at the end is half the middle value.","n = 20 turns per cm = 2000 per m, I = 2 A: B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2000 \xD7 2 = 5.03 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T, and 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T at an end.","mgsol"),e("Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",1,"Toroid","B = {\u03BC\u2080NI;2\u03C0r} inside the ring, r from the centre of the ring \xB7 B = 0 in the hole and outside \xB7 for a thin ring of mean radius R: B \u2248 \u03BC\u2080nI with n = {N;2\u03C0R} \xB7 the field varies as {1;r} across the section","Take a circle of radius r through the ring: B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080NI. The field is larger on the inner side of the ring.","N = 500 turns, I = 2 A, mean radius 10 cm: B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 500 \xD7 2 \xF7 0.1 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T."),e("Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",2,"Solenoid problems and winding wire","a wire of length \u2113 wound on a tube of radius R: N = {\u2113;2\u03C0R} turns \xB7 changing the length with the same wire and current changes n \xB7 two solenoids put end to end add their fields \xB7 fields from a solenoid and a straight wire add as vectors (they are perpendicular)","Write N or n in terms of the data first. Then put it into \u03BC\u2080nI.","A tube of radius 2 cm, length 20 cm is wound with 25.1 m of wire in one layer: N = 25.1 \xF7 (2\u03C0 \xD7 0.02) = 200 turns, n = 1000 per metre. With I = 1 A, B = 1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T."),e("Force on moving charges and on wires",1,"Force on a moving charge","F = q(v \xD7 B) = qvB sin\u03B8 \xB7 direction by the right-hand rule (reverse for a negative charge) \xB7 F \u27C2 v, so the force does no work and the speed and kinetic energy do not change \xB7 no force if v \u2225 B or v = 0 \xB7 with both fields: F = q(E + v \xD7 B), the Lorentz force","Find the component of v across B. The force is qvB for that component, perpendicular to both. Never write that the magnetic field changes the energy.","A proton at 2 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s perpendicular to B = 0.5 T feels F = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 0.5 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 N."),e("Force on moving charges and on wires",1,"Force on a current-carrying wire","F = I (L \xD7 B) = BIL sin\u03B8 \xB7 direction by the right-hand rule on L \xD7 B \xB7 F = 0 if the wire is parallel to B \xB7 curved wire: F = I (L_eff \xD7 B), where L_eff is the straight line from the start to the end \xB7 a closed loop in a uniform field: net force = 0","For a bent wire in a uniform field, replace it by the straight chord joining its ends. A semicircle of radius R has L_eff = 2R.","I = 5 A, L = 0.4 m, B = 0.3 T at 30\xB0: F = 5 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 0.3 \xD7 0.5 = 0.3 N. A semicircular wire of R = 10 cm with the same I and B = 0.4 T (field in the plane of the loop, at right angles to the chord): F = 2 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 5 \xD7 0.4 = 0.4 N."),e("Force on moving charges and on wires",1,"Force between parallel wires","F per unit length = {\u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082;2\u03C0d} = {2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I\u2081I\u2082;d} \xB7 currents in the same direction attract; opposite directions repel \xB7 each wire feels the same force (Newton\u2019s third law) \xB7 the field of one wire at the other is B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/2\u03C0d and F = B\u2081I\u2082\u2113","Take the field of one wire at the position of the other and then the force on a length \u2113 of it. Check the direction by the rule: parallel currents attract.","Two wires 10 cm apart, both 5 A in the same direction: F/\u2113 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 25 \xF7 0.1 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N/m, attraction."),e("Force on moving charges and on wires",1,"Definition of the ampere","one ampere is the steady current that, in two long parallel wires 1 m apart in vacuum, gives a force of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N per metre \xB7 this fixes \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m/A in the older SI \xB7 one coulomb is one ampere second","Put I\u2081 = I\u2082 = 1 A and d = 1 m in the formula for parallel wires.","F/\u2113 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1 \xD7 1 \xF7 1 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N/m."),e("Force on moving charges and on wires",2,"Wire in the field of another wire or loop","a straight wire near a long wire: the force is found by integrating B over its length \xB7 a rigid wire parallel to a long wire: F = B \u2113 I, B taken at its distance \xB7 a loop near a wire: the sides parallel to the wire attract or repel with different B, so there is a net force towards the wire if the near side has the same direction of current","Calculate the force on each side separately (the two sides perpendicular to the wire cancel), then add the signed forces.","Square loop of side 10 cm, I = 5 A, with a side parallel to a long wire carrying 10 A at 10 cm. The near side feels 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 5 \xF7 0.1 \xD7 0.1 = 10\u207B\u2075 N, the far side (20 cm) feels 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N the other way. Net force 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N."),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Circular motion in a magnetic field","v \u27C2 B: r = {mv;qB} = {p;qB} = {\u221A(2mK);qB} = {1;B}\u221A({2mV;q}) \xB7 \u03C9 = {qB;m}, T = {2\u03C0m;qB}, f = {qB;2\u03C0m} \xB7 T and f do not depend on v or r \xB7 the speed is constant; the charge goes anticlockwise or clockwise by sign","Pick the form of r that fits the data: v, p, K or V. Remember that T is the same for all speeds.","An electron accelerated through 100 V enters B = 1 mT at right angles: v = 5.93 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s, r = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 5.93 \xD7 10\u2076 \xF7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 3.4 cm."),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Comparing particles in the same field","same v: r \u221D {m;q} \xB7 same K: r \u221D {\u221Am;q} \xB7 same V: r \u221D \u221A({m;q}) \xB7 same p: r \u221D {1;q} \xB7 T \u221D {m;q} \xB7 proton, deuteron, \u03B1 in the same B and V: radii in the ratio 1 : \u221A2 : \u221A2 \xB7 same K for proton and \u03B1: r is the same (\u221A4/2 = 1)","Write r in terms of the quantity that is common. Do not use r = mv/qB when the energy or the voltage is given: it hides the mass dependence.","Proton and \u03B1 particle enter the same B with the same K: r_p : r_\u03B1 = (1/1) : (\u221A4/2) = 1 : 1. With the same V it is 1 : \u221A2."),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Helical motion","v makes an angle \u03B8 with B: v\u2225 = v cos\u03B8 (unchanged), v\u22A5 = v sin\u03B8 (circles) \xB7 radius r = {mv sin\u03B8;qB} \xB7 pitch p = v\u2225T = {2\u03C0mv cos\u03B8;qB} \xB7 the path is a helix along B \xB7 \u03B8 = 90\xB0 gives a circle, \u03B8 = 0\xB0 a straight line","Split v along and across B. The radius uses v\u22A5, the pitch uses v\u2225 \xD7 T.","Proton at 10\u2076 m/s, \u03B8 = 60\xB0 to B = 0.1 T: r = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 0.866 \xF7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.1) = 9.0 cm and pitch = 2\u03C0 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 0.5 \xF7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070) = 32.8 cm.","mghel"),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Deflection in a region of width d","charge enters perpendicular to B in a strip of width d: sin\u03B8 = {d;r} \xB7 deflection angle \u03B8, time in the field t = {\u03B8;\u03C9} = {\u03B8m;qB} \xB7 if d \u2265 r the particle turns back through a semicircle \xB7 momentum change = 2mv sin{\u03B8;2}","Find r first. Compare d with r. Then sin\u03B8 = d/r gives the angle turned and t = (\u03B8/2\u03C0)T.","A proton of r = 10 cm in B = 0.1 T crosses a strip 5 cm wide: sin\u03B8 = 0.5, \u03B8 = 30\xB0. The time is (\u03C0/6) \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xF7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.1) = 5.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s."),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Velocity selector","for undeflected motion qE = qvB, so v = {E;B} \xB7 E \u27C2 B \u27C2 v \xB7 the charge and the mass do not matter \xB7 faster particles bend one way, slower ones the other way \xB7 used before a mass spectrometer, where r = {mv;qB} gives m","Equate the electric and the magnetic force. If E and B are given, v = E/B at once.","E = 3000 V/m and B = 0.01 T pass particles of v = 3000 \xF7 0.01 = 3 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s.","mgvs"),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",1,"Cyclotron","the particle goes round in the dees with f = {qB;2\u03C0m}, the same for every orbit, so the oscillator frequency is fixed \xB7 final speed v = {qBR;m}, K = {q\xB2B\xB2R\xB2;2m} \xB7 K does not depend on the dee voltage; the voltage sets only the number of turns {K;2qV} \xB7 not suited to electrons (relativistic mass change)","Find f from B and the mass. For the energy, put R of the last orbit in K = q\xB2B\xB2R\xB2/2m.","Proton, B = 1 T: f = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xF7 (2\u03C0 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077) = 15.2 MHz. With R = 0.5 m, K = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)\xB2 \xD7 1 \xD7 0.25 \xF7 (2 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077) = 1.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 J = 12 MeV."),e("Motion in fields: circle, helix, cyclotron, selector",2,"Cycloid and crossed fields","E \u27C2 B and a charge released at rest: the path is a cycloid with drift speed {E;B} \xB7 maximum speed 2E/B \xB7 the net force on a charge moving at v = E/B is zero \xB7 a charge crossing a region of E and B with qE \u2260 qvB bends towards the larger force","Write the net force qE + qv \xD7 B. If it is zero the motion is straight; otherwise find which force is larger.","E = 600 V/m, B = 0.2 T: the drift speed is 3000 m/s and the highest speed of a particle released from rest is 6000 m/s."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",1,"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment","m = NIA (direction by the right-hand rule on the current, normal to the loop) \xB7 \u03C4 = m \xD7 B, \u03C4 = NIAB sin\u03B8 with \u03B8 the angle between the normal and B \xB7 the net force on the loop is zero \xB7 a revolving charge: m = {q;2m}L = {1;2}q\u03C9r\xB2 \xB7 a loop turns so that its moment lines up with B","Find m first, then \u03B8 between the normal of the loop (not its plane) and B. If the plane is parallel to B, \u03B8 = 90\xB0 and \u03C4 is largest.","100 turns, area 0.02 m\xB2, I = 0.5 A, B = 0.2 T with the plane parallel to B: \u03C4 = 100 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 0.02 \xD7 0.2 = 0.2 N m."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",1,"Dipole in a magnetic field: energy and work","U = \u2212m\xB7B = \u2212mB cos\u03B8 \xB7 stable at \u03B8 = 0 (U = \u2212mB), unstable at \u03B8 = 180\xB0 (U = +mB) \xB7 work to turn from \u03B8\u2081 to \u03B8\u2082: W = mB(cos\u03B8\u2081 \u2212 cos\u03B8\u2082) \xB7 from 0 to 180\xB0: W = 2mB \xB7 \u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8","Find the angle between m and B in the start and end positions. The work done by an outside agent equals the change of U.","m = 3 A m\xB2, B = 0.2 T, \u03B8 = 30\xB0: \u03C4 = 0.30 N m and U = \u22120.52 J. Turning from 0\xB0 to 180\xB0 needs 2 \xD7 3 \xD7 0.2 = 1.2 J."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",1,"Magnetic moment of an orbiting electron","\u03BC = {e;2m}L = {evr;2} \xB7 L = {nh;2\u03C0} in Bohr\u2019s model \xB7 Bohr magneton \u03BC_B = {eh;4\u03C0m} = 9.27 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 A m\xB2 \xB7 gyromagnetic ratio {\u03BC;L} = {e;2m} = 8.8 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 C/kg \xB7 the moment is opposite to L for the electron (negative charge)","Write I = e/T and A = \u03C0r\xB2. Then \u03BC = IA = evr/2 = (e/2m)L.","Hydrogen ground state (n = 1): \u03BC = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xF7 (4\u03C0 \xD7 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9) = 9.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 A m\xB2."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",1,"Moving coil galvanometer","NIAB = k\u03B8 (radial field keeps the torque equal to NIAB at every angle) \xB7 current sensitivity {\u03B8;I} = {NAB;k} \xB7 voltage sensitivity {\u03B8;V} = {NAB;kR} \xB7 I = {k;NAB}\u03B8, a uniform scale \xB7 more turns N: current sensitivity \u221D N but resistance also \u221D N, so voltage sensitivity stays the same","Write the balance of the magnetic torque and the spring torque. Make a galvanometer more sensitive by raising N, A, B and lowering k.","A coil with N = 100, A = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, B = 0.2 T and k = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N m/rad turns by \u03B8/I = 100 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.2 \xF7 (4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 10\u2074 rad/A."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",1,"Conversion to ammeter and voltmeter","ammeter: a small shunt S in parallel, S = {I_gG;I \u2212 I_g} \xB7 resistance of the ammeter = {GS;G + S} (very small) \xB7 voltmeter: a large series resistance R = {V;I_g} \u2212 G \xB7 resistance of the voltmeter = G + R (very large) \xB7 range grows by the shunt or series resistance","Ig and G are the full-scale current and the resistance of the galvanometer. The shunt carries the extra current I \u2212 Ig, with the same voltage IgG.","G = 50 \u03A9, Ig = 2 mA. For 0 to 1 A: S = 0.002 \xD7 50 \xF7 0.998 = 0.10 \u03A9. For 0 to 10 V: R = 10 \xF7 0.002 \u2212 50 = 4950 \u03A9 in series."),e("Torque on a loop and the galvanometer",2,"Tangent galvanometer","B_H tan\u03B8 = {\u03BC\u2080NI;2R} so I = {2RB_H;\u03BC\u2080N} tan\u03B8 = K tan\u03B8 \xB7 reduction factor K = {2RB_H;\u03BC\u2080N} \xB7 most sensitive near \u03B8 = 45\xB0 \xB7 coil plane set along the magnetic meridian","Put the coil in the meridian so that its field is perpendicular to B_H. Then tan\u03B8 = B_coil/B_H.","N = 50, R = 10 cm, B_H = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T: K = 2 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xF7 (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 50) = 0.0955 A, so a deflection of 45\xB0 means a current of 0.0955 A."),e("Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole",1,"Field of a short bar magnet","axial (end-on): B = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{2m;r\xB3} \xB7 equatorial (broadside): B = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{m;r\xB3} \xB7 axial is twice equatorial for the same r \xB7 equatorial B is opposite to m, axial is along m \xB7 general point at angle \u03B8 from the axis: B = {\u03BC\u2080m;4\u03C0r\xB3}\u221A(1 + 3cos\xB2\u03B8), tan \u03B1 = {1;2}tan\u03B8 \xB7 r is measured from the centre and r \u226B length","Quote the point as axial or equatorial first. Use r from the centre of the magnet. The same formulas hold for a small current loop of moment NIA.","m = 2 A m\xB2 at r = 0.2 m: axial B = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 2 \xF7 0.008 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T and equatorial B = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.","mgbar"),e("Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole",1,"Poles, moment and dividing a magnet","m = (pole strength) \xD7 (length 2l) from S to N \xB7 force between poles F = {\u03BC\u2080;4\u03C0}{m\u2081m\u2082;r\xB2} \xB7 a magnet cut in half across its length: m halves (strength same, length half) \xB7 cut along its length: m halves (strength halves) \xB7 the poles never exist alone \xB7 bar magnet \u2248 solenoid with the same m","Start with m = qm \xD7 2l and apply the cut. The pole strength is set by the area and the magnetisation, so it does not change when the length is cut.","A magnet of moment 4 A m\xB2 is cut into two equal pieces across its length: each piece has m = 2 A m\xB2. The same magnet cut along its length gives two pieces of 2 A m\xB2."),e("Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole",1,"Torque and energy of a bar magnet","\u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8 \xB7 U = \u2212mB cos\u03B8 \xB7 W = mB(cos\u03B8\u2081 \u2212 cos\u03B8\u2082) \xB7 a magnet in equilibrium lies along B (stable) \xB7 when two magnets are put together, add the moments as vectors","Treat the magnet exactly as a loop: the moment is m, the angle is between m and B.","A magnet of m = 0.5 A m\xB2 in B = 0.4 T at 60\xB0: \u03C4 = 0.5 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 0.866 = 0.173 N m."),e("Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole",1,"Oscillation of a magnet","T = 2\u03C0\u221A({I;mB}) for small angles \xB7 T \u221D {1;\u221AB} and T \u221D \u221AI \xB7 f = {1;2\u03C0}\u221A({mB;I}) \xB7 two magnets stuck together in the same sense: T = 2\u03C0\u221A({I\u2081 + I\u2082;(m\u2081 + m\u2082)B}); with one reversed use (m\u2081 \u2212 m\u2082) \xB7 B \u221D {1;T\xB2}","The restoring torque is mB sin\u03B8 \u2248 mB\u03B8, so the motion is simple harmonic with \u03C9\xB2 = mB/I. Use B_H for a magnet in the earth\u2019s field.","m = 2 A m\xB2, I = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 kg m\xB2, B = 0.1 T: T = 2\u03C0\u221A(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xF7 0.2) = 0.0628 s. With B four times larger, T = 0.0314 s."),e("Bar magnet as a magnetic dipole",2,"Vibration magnetometer and null points","compare two fields: {B\u2081;B\u2082} = {T\u2082\xB2;T\u2081\xB2} for the same magnet \xB7 neutral point: the field of the magnet cancels B_H (on the equatorial line for N pointing north, on the axis for N pointing south) \xB7 tan position: with the magnet\u2019s axis east\u2013west (tan A position, point on the equatorial line), tan\u03B8 = {B_magnet;B_H}","Use the same m and I; only B changes, so T\xB2 \u221D 1/B. For a null point set the magnet field equal and opposite to B_H and solve for r.","A magnet makes 10 oscillations in 20 s in the field B\u2081 and in 40 s in field B\u2082 (T\u2082 = 2T\u2081): B\u2081 : B\u2082 = 4 : 1."),e("Earth\u2019s magnetism",1,"Elements of the earth\u2019s field","B_H = B cos\u03B4 \xB7 B_V = B sin\u03B4 \xB7 tan\u03B4 = {B_V;B_H} \xB7 B = \u221A(B_H\xB2 + B_V\xB2) \xB7 declination: angle between the geographic and the magnetic meridian \xB7 dip \u03B4: angle of B with the horizontal \xB7 typical B_H \u2248 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, B \u2248 3\u20136 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T \xB7 \u03B4 = 0\xB0 at the magnetic equator and 90\xB0 at the magnetic poles","Draw the right triangle B_H, B_V, B. Any two of the elements give the others.","B_H = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T and \u03B4 = 60\xB0: B = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xF7 0.5 = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T and B_V = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 tan 60\xB0 = 5.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.","mgearth"),e("Earth\u2019s magnetism",1,"Apparent dip","in a vertical plane at angle \u03B1 to the magnetic meridian: tan \u03B4\u2032 = {tan \u03B4;cos \u03B1} \xB7 horizontal component there: B_H cos \u03B1 \xB7 \u03B1 = 90\xB0: \u03B4\u2032 = 90\xB0 (a needle stands vertical) \xB7 \u03B1 = 0\xB0 gives the true dip \xB7 apparent dip is always greater than or equal to the true dip","The vertical component stays the same. Only the horizontal component along the new plane is B_H cos \u03B1.","\u03B4 = 45\xB0 and \u03B1 = 60\xB0: tan \u03B4\u2032 = 1 \xF7 0.5 = 2, so \u03B4\u2032 = 63.4\xB0."),e("Earth\u2019s magnetism",2,"Earth as a dipole and field in magnets","the earth\u2019s field is like that of a dipole whose south pole lies near the geographic north \xB7 the magnetic north of a needle points to the geographic north \xB7 field lines enter the earth in the northern hemisphere (dip downward) \xB7 the magnetic equator is where \u03B4 = 0","Keep the naming straight: a compass needle\u2019s N-pole points north because the earth\u2019s magnetic pole in the north is a magnetic south pole.","At the magnetic equator B_V = 0 and B = B_H. At a magnetic pole B_H = 0 and a dip needle stands vertical."),e("Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",1,"Gauss\u2019s law for magnetism","\u222EB\xB7dA = 0 for any closed surface \xB7 no isolated magnetic poles (monopoles) exist \xB7 field lines are closed curves with no beginning or end \xB7 the flux entering a closed surface equals the flux leaving it \xB7 flux \u03A6 = B A cos\u03B8 through a flat area","Use it to check a claim: if a question asks for the flux of B through a closed surface, the answer is zero. For an open surface use \u03A6 = BA cos\u03B8.","Flux of a uniform field of 0.2 T through a loop of area 0.05 m\xB2 with the normal at 60\xB0 to B: \u03A6 = 0.2 \xD7 0.05 \xD7 0.5 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Wb. Through a closed box it is 0."),e("Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",1,"H, M, \u03C7 and \u03BCr","B = \u03BC\u2080(H + M) = \u03BCH \xB7 M = \u03C7H (M is the moment per unit volume) \xB7 \u03BCr = 1 + \u03C7 \xB7 \u03BC = \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr \xB7 for a solenoid H = nI \xB7 with a core B = \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr nI \xB7 \u03C7 is dimensionless; H and M have unit A/m","H comes only from the free current (nI); M is the response of the material. Use B = \u03BC\u2080(1 + \u03C7)H for a quick answer.","Solenoid n = 1000 per metre, I = 0.5 A, iron core \u03BCr = 500: B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 500 \xD7 1000 \xD7 0.5 = 0.314 T. A material with \u03C7 = 299 has \u03BCr = 300."),e("Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",1,"Dia, para and ferro magnetism","diamagnetic: \u03C7 small and negative (about \u221210\u207B\u2075), \u03BCr slightly below 1, pushed out of the field, independent of temperature (a superconductor has \u03C7 = \u22121) \xB7 paramagnetic: \u03C7 small and positive (10\u207B\u2075 to 10\u207B\xB3), \u03BCr slightly above 1, weakly drawn in, Curie\u2019s law \u03C7 = {C;T} \xB7 ferromagnetic: \u03C7 very large (10\xB2 to 10\u2075), strongly drawn in, domains, above the Curie temperature it becomes paramagnetic with \u03C7 = {C;T \u2212 T_c}","Classify by the sign and size of \u03C7. For temperature changes use \u03C7 \u221D 1/T for para and \u03C7 \u221D 1/(T \u2212 T_c) for ferro above T_c.","\u03C7 of a paramagnetic salt is 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 at 300 K; at 400 K it is 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 300 \xF7 400 = 2.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3."),e("Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",1,"Hysteresis and uses of materials","retentivity: B left when H returns to zero \xB7 coercivity: reverse H needed to bring B to zero \xB7 the area of the B\u2013H loop is the energy lost as heat per unit volume per cycle \xB7 soft iron: small coercivity, small loop, high \u03BC, used in electromagnets, transformer cores and electric motors \xB7 steel and alnico: large retentivity and coercivity, used for permanent magnets","Match the use with the loop: tall and thin for cores that are magnetised again and again, wide for permanent magnets.","A core taken through 50 cycles per second with a loop area (B\u2013H) of 400 J/m\xB3 per cycle loses 400 \xD7 50 = 2 \xD7 10\u2074 W per cubic metre."),e("Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",2,"Electromagnets, Curie point and magnetisation","an electromagnet is a solenoid with a soft iron core: strong, switchable \xB7 B = \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr nI is far larger than without the core \xB7 heating a ferromagnet above the Curie temperature (iron about 1040 K) makes it paramagnetic \xB7 saturation: M reaches its maximum when all domains are aligned \xB7 a bar magnet in a non-uniform field is drawn towards the stronger field if para- or ferromagnetic and pushed out if diamagnetic","Use B = \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr nI to compare an air-core and an iron-core solenoid. The ratio is \u03BCr.","A solenoid with 2000 turns per metre carries 1 A. The field is 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T in air and 0.5 T with a core of \u03BCr = 200, an increase of 200 times.")],look:["Biot\u2013Savart dB = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) I dl sin\u03B8/r\xB2; wire 2\xD710\u207B\u2077I/d; loop centre \u03BC\u2080NI/2R; arc \u03BC\u2080I\u03B8/4\u03C0R; finite wire (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(sin\u03B1 + sin\u03B2)","Loop axis \u03BC\u2080NIR\xB2/2(R\xB2+x\xB2)^(3/2); solenoid \u03BC\u2080nI (end \u03BC\u2080nI/2); toroid \u03BC\u2080NI/2\u03C0r; cylinder inside \u03BC\u2080Ir/2\u03C0a\xB2","Ampere \u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080I_enc; Gauss \u222EB\xB7dA = 0; no monopoles; parallel wires 2\xD710\u207B\u2077I\u2081I\u2082/d per metre, same direction attract","F = qvB sin\u03B8 does no work; F = BIL sin\u03B8; bent wire: use the chord; closed loop: net force 0; Lorentz F = q(E + v \xD7 B)","r = mv/qB = p/qB = \u221A(2mK)/qB; T = 2\u03C0m/qB (independent of v); helix: r uses v sin\u03B8, pitch uses v cos\u03B8","Selector v = E/B; cyclotron f = qB/2\u03C0m, K = q\xB2B\xB2R\xB2/2m; same K: r \u221D \u221Am/q; same V: r \u221D \u221A(m/q)","m = NIA; \u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8; U = \u2212mB cos\u03B8; W = mB(cos\u03B8\u2081 \u2212 cos\u03B8\u2082); \u03BC = (e/2m)L; \u03BC_B = 9.27\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2074 A m\xB2","Galvanometer NIAB = k\u03B8; shunt S = IgG/(I \u2212 Ig); series R = V/Ig \u2212 G; more turns leaves voltage sensitivity unchanged","Bar magnet axial (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)2m/r\xB3, equatorial (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)m/r\xB3; T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/mB); cut in half: m halves; B \u221D 1/T\xB2","Earth: B_H = B cos\u03B4, B_V = B sin\u03B4, tan\u03B4 = B_V/B_H, tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4/cos\u03B1; dia \u03C7 < 0, para \u03C7 \u221D 1/T, ferro large \u03C7; soft iron for cores, steel for magnets"],notes:[{h:"Biot\u2013Savart law and simple currents",items:["The field of a current element falls as 1/r\xB2 and is perpendicular to both dl and r.","A long wire gives circles of B around it with B \u221D 1/d.","A finite wire needs the two angles from the perpendicular: B \u221D sin\u03B1 + sin\u03B2.","At the centre of a loop B = \u03BC\u2080NI/2R; an arc gives the fraction \u03B8/2\u03C0 of it.","A wire whose line passes through the point gives no field there.","The right-hand rule gives the direction: thumb along I, fingers along B."],fig:"mgwire"},{h:"Ampere\u2019s law, solenoid and toroid",items:["\u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080I_enc is true for every closed path and is useful only with symmetry.","Inside a solid cylinder B grows linearly with r; outside it falls as 1/r.","A long solenoid has a uniform B = \u03BC\u2080nI inside and almost zero outside.","At the end of a solenoid the field is half the middle value.","A toroid has B = \u03BC\u2080NI/2\u03C0r inside the ring and zero in the hole and outside.","An iron core multiplies the field by \u03BCr."],fig:"mgsol"},{h:"Force on a moving charge",items:["F = qvB sin\u03B8 is perpendicular to v, so the speed and K do not change.","No force acts if the charge is at rest or moves along B.","Reverse the direction of the force for a negative charge.","A magnetic field cannot do work; an electric field can.","With both fields the Lorentz force is q(E + v \xD7 B).","Two charges of equal q and opposite sign curve in opposite senses."]},{h:"Force on wires and between wires",items:["F = BIL sin\u03B8; the direction follows L \xD7 B.","For a curved wire in a uniform field use the straight chord between its ends.","A closed current loop feels no net force in a uniform field.","Parallel currents attract, antiparallel currents repel.","F/\u2113 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/2\u03C0d = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I\u2081I\u2082/d in newton per metre.","This force defines the ampere: 1 A in wires 1 m apart gives 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N/m."]},{h:"Circles, helices and the velocity selector",items:["A charge across B moves on a circle with r = mv/qB and T = 2\u03C0m/qB.","T does not depend on the speed or the radius.","The pitch of a helix is v cos\u03B8 \xD7 T and the radius uses v sin\u03B8.","For the same K or V write r in K or V, not in v.","A velocity selector passes v = E/B whatever q and m are.","A mass spectrometer measures r = mv/qB to find the mass."],fig:"mghel"},{h:"Cyclotron",items:["The dees use a fixed frequency f = qB/2\u03C0m.","The particle is accelerated each time it crosses the gap.","The final speed is qBR/m and does not depend on the voltage.","The voltage only sets how many turns are needed: K/2qV.","It cannot accelerate electrons (their mass changes at high speed).","The magnetic field only bends the path; the electric field gives the energy."]},{h:"Loop, torque and galvanometer",items:["A loop has a moment m = NIA along its normal.","\u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8 with \u03B8 between m and B; the force on the loop is zero.","The energy is U = \u2212mB cos\u03B8: lowest when m is along B.","A radial field makes the torque independent of the angle and the scale uniform.","Shunt: small and in parallel (ammeter). Multiplier: large and in series (voltmeter).","An ideal ammeter has zero resistance and an ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance."]},{h:"Bar magnet and the dipole field",items:["Axial B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)2m/r\xB3; equatorial B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)m/r\xB3.","The axial value is twice the equatorial one for the same distance.","On the equator B is opposite to m; on the axis it is along m.","Cutting a magnet in half across its length halves m but keeps each piece a full magnet.","\u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8 and T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/mB) for small oscillations.","A bar magnet behaves like a solenoid of the same moment; its lines are closed inside and outside."],fig:"mgbar"},{h:"Earth\u2019s magnetism",items:["B_H = B cos\u03B4 and B_V = B sin\u03B4; tan\u03B4 = B_V/B_H.","Declination is the angle between the geographic and magnetic meridians.","The dip is 0\xB0 at the magnetic equator and 90\xB0 at the magnetic poles.","In a vertical plane at angle \u03B1 to the meridian, tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4/cos\u03B1.","A tangent galvanometer works on B_coil = B_H tan\u03B8.","A compass needle\u2019s north end points to the earth\u2019s magnetic south pole in the north."],fig:"mgearth"},{h:"Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",items:["\u222EB\xB7dA = 0: there are no magnetic monopoles.","Dia: \u03C7 < 0 and small; para: \u03C7 > 0 and small, \u03C7 \u221D 1/T; ferro: \u03C7 very large.","B = \u03BC\u2080(H + M), M = \u03C7H and \u03BCr = 1 + \u03C7.","Above the Curie temperature a ferromagnet becomes paramagnetic.","The hysteresis loop area is the energy lost per unit volume per cycle.","Soft iron for electromagnets and cores; steel for permanent magnets."]}],asked:[{t:"Field of a straight wire, finite or infinite",what:"A long or a finite wire carries a current. Find B at a point, or the distance at which B has a stated value.",steps:["Infinite wire: B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I/d.","Finite wire: B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(sin\u03B1 + sin\u03B2).","Use the right-hand rule for the direction."],ex:"10 A, 5 cm: 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.",tp:"field",fig:"mgwire"},{t:"Centre of loops, arcs and combinations",what:"A wire is bent into a circle, semicircle or arcs joined by straight pieces. Find B at the centre.",steps:["Arc: B = \u03BC\u2080I\u03B8/4\u03C0R.","Straight pieces on a line through the centre give zero.","Add the arcs as vectors."],ex:"Semicircle R = 20 cm, 5 A: 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.",tp:"field"},{t:"Field on the axis of a loop or a coil",what:"A coil of N turns carries a current. Find B at a distance x on its axis, or the ratio to the value at the centre.",steps:["B = \u03BC\u2080NIR\xB2/2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2).","Ratio to centre: [R\xB2/(R\xB2 + x\xB2)]^(3/2).","Far away B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2m/x\xB3)."],ex:"R = 3 cm, x = 4 cm: 0.216 of the centre value.",tp:"field"},{t:"Ampere\u2019s law for cylinders and sheets",what:"A thick wire or a conducting tube has a current. Find B inside and outside, or where B is the same.",steps:["Choose a circular path of radius r.","I_enc = I r\xB2/a\xB2 inside a solid cylinder.","Outside B = \u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r."],ex:"10 A, a = 2 cm, r = 1 cm: 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.",tp:"field"},{t:"Solenoid and toroid",what:"The turns, the length and the current of a solenoid or toroid are given. Find B inside, at the end or in terms of the radius.",steps:["n = N/L in turns per metre.","Solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080nI, end half of it.","Toroid B = \u03BC\u2080NI/2\u03C0r."],ex:"2000 per m, 2 A: 5.03 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T.",tp:"field",fig:"mgsol"},{t:"Force on a charge in a magnetic field",what:"A charge enters a magnetic field with a given velocity. Find the force, its direction or the acceleration.",steps:["Take the component of v across B.","F = qvB sin\u03B8.","Direction by v \xD7 B, reversed for a negative charge."],ex:"Proton, 2 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s, 0.5 T: 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 N.",tp:"force"},{t:"Force on a straight or bent wire",what:"A wire of given length and shape carries a current in a uniform field. Find the force on it.",steps:["F = BIL sin\u03B8 for a straight piece.","Bent wire: use the chord between the ends.","Closed loop: net force zero."],ex:"5 A, 0.4 m, 0.3 T, 30\xB0: 0.3 N.",tp:"force"},{t:"Force between parallel wires and the ampere",what:"Two long wires carry given currents at a given distance. Find the force per metre or the place where the net field or force is zero.",steps:["F/\u2113 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I\u2081I\u2082/d.","Same direction: attract.","Zero field lies between the wires only if the currents are in the same direction."],ex:"5 A and 5 A at 10 cm: 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N/m.",tp:"force"},{t:"Radius and period of circular motion",what:"A particle enters a field at right angles with a given speed, K or V. Find r, T or f, or compare two particles.",steps:["r = mv/qB = \u221A(2mK)/qB.","T = 2\u03C0m/qB.","Same K: r \u221D \u221Am/q; same V: r \u221D \u221A(m/q)."],ex:"Electron, 100 V, 1 mT: 3.4 cm.",tp:"motion"},{t:"Helical motion",what:"The velocity makes an angle with B. Find the radius, the pitch or the time for one turn.",steps:["v\u2225 = v cos\u03B8, v\u22A5 = v sin\u03B8.","r = mv sin\u03B8/qB.","Pitch = v\u2225 \xD7 T."],ex:"Proton, 10\u2076 m/s, 60\xB0, 0.1 T: r = 9.0 cm, pitch = 32.8 cm.",tp:"motion",fig:"mghel"},{t:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",what:"E and B are crossed. Find the speed of the undeflected charge, or the path of one with another speed.",steps:["qE = qvB.","v = E/B.","Larger force decides the side of bending."],ex:"3000 V/m, 0.01 T: 3 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s.",tp:"motion",fig:"mgvs"},{t:"Cyclotron frequency and energy",what:"The field and the radius of a cyclotron are given. Find the frequency, the final speed or the energy.",steps:["f = qB/2\u03C0m.","v = qBR/m.","K = q\xB2B\xB2R\xB2/2m."],ex:"Proton, 1 T, 0.5 m: 15.2 MHz, 12 MeV.",tp:"motion"},{t:"Torque and magnetic moment of a loop",what:"A coil is placed in a field at a given angle. Find the moment, the torque, the energy or the work to turn it.",steps:["m = NIA along the normal.","\u03C4 = mB sin\u03B8.","U = \u2212mB cos\u03B8."],ex:"3 A m\xB2, 0.2 T, 30\xB0: 0.30 N m.",tp:"force"},{t:"Magnetic moment of a revolving charge",what:"An electron or a charged ring goes round. Find the current, the moment or the ratio of the moment to the angular momentum.",steps:["I = q/T.","\u03BC = IA = (q/2m)L.","Bohr model: L = nh/2\u03C0."],ex:"Hydrogen n = 1: 9.27 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 A m\xB2.",tp:"bar"},{t:"Galvanometer sensitivity",what:"A coil of given N, A, B and k is given. Find the deflection per unit current or voltage, or the effect of changing N.",steps:["NIAB = k\u03B8.","\u03B8/I = NAB/k.","\u03B8/V = NAB/kR."],ex:"N = 100, 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, 0.2 T, 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076: 10\u2074 rad/A.",tp:"galv"},{t:"Ammeter and voltmeter from a galvanometer",what:"G and Ig are given. Find the shunt for a range of current or the series resistance for a range of voltage.",steps:["S = IgG/(I \u2212 Ig).","R = V/Ig \u2212 G.","Combined resistance GS/(G + S) or G + R."],ex:"50 \u03A9, 2 mA: S = 0.10 \u03A9, R = 4950 \u03A9.",tp:"galv"},{t:"Field of a bar magnet or a small loop",what:"A short magnet has a moment m. Find B at a point on the axis or on the equatorial line, or the ratio of the two.",steps:["Axial (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)2m/r\xB3.","Equatorial (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)m/r\xB3.","General angle: \u221A(1 + 3cos\xB2\u03B8)."],ex:"2 A m\xB2, 0.2 m: 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T and 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.",tp:"bar",fig:"mgbar"},{t:"Time period of a magnet in a field",what:"A magnet vibrates in a field. Find T, compare two fields or two magnets, or find B_H from the oscillations.",steps:["T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/mB).","B \u221D 1/T\xB2.","Add I and m for a combination."],ex:"2 A m\xB2, 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 kg m\xB2, 0.1 T: 0.0628 s.",tp:"osc"},{t:"Earth\u2019s magnetic elements",what:"Two of B, B_H, B_V and the dip are given. Find the others.",steps:["B_H = B cos\u03B4.","B_V = B sin\u03B4.","tan\u03B4 = B_V/B_H."],ex:"B_H = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, 60\xB0: B = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.",tp:"earth",fig:"mgearth"},{t:"Apparent dip and tangent galvanometer",what:"A dip needle is in a plane at an angle to the meridian, or a coil is in the meridian. Find the apparent dip or the current.",steps:["tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4/cos\u03B1.","B_coil = B_H tan\u03B8.","I = K tan\u03B8 with K = 2RB_H/\u03BC\u2080N."],ex:"45\xB0, \u03B1 = 60\xB0: 63.4\xB0.",tp:"earth"},{t:"Dia, para and ferromagnetic materials",what:"Values of \u03C7, \u03BCr or the behaviour in a field or with temperature are given. Classify the material.",steps:["Dia: \u03C7 < 0, para: \u03C7 > 0 small, ferro: large \u03C7.","Para \u03C7 \u221D 1/T.","Ferro above T_c becomes para."],ex:"3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 at 300 K gives 2.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 at 400 K.",tp:"mat"},{t:"Hysteresis, electromagnets and permanent magnets",what:"The loop of a material is described. Say which is suitable for a core or a permanent magnet, or find the energy lost.",steps:["Retentivity and coercivity from the loop.","Loop area = energy loss per cycle per volume.","Soft iron for cores, steel for magnets."],ex:"400 J/m\xB3 per cycle, 50 Hz: 2 \xD7 10\u2074 W/m\xB3.",tp:"mat"}],guide:{intro:"Magnetic Effects of Current and Magnetism gives about 3 questions in JEE Mains every year, mostly short numericals and statements. The ones that come most are the field of wires, loops and solenoids, the radius and the helix of a charged particle, the torque and energy of a loop or a magnet, the galvanometer and its conversions, and the elements of the earth\u2019s field. Learn the formulas of the fields and the motion with their directions, and use about 8 hours over 5 days.",plan:[["Day 1 \xB7 90 min","Biot\u2013Savart law, straight wire, arcs and loops, the axis of a loop, polygons and rotating charge."],["Day 2 \xB7 90 min","Ampere\u2019s law, cylinder, solenoid, toroid, then the force on a charge and on wires, parallel wires and the ampere."],["Day 3 \xB7 100 min","Circle, helix, strip deflection, velocity selector, cyclotron, then comparing particles."],["Day 4 \xB7 90 min","Torque and energy of a loop, galvanometer, shunt and series resistance, bar magnet field and oscillation."],["Day 5 \xB7 80 min","Earth\u2019s magnetism, dia/para/ferro, hysteresis, Gauss\u2019s law; then the Most asked types and a mock test."]],basics:["Vectors and the cross product with the right-hand rule.","Circular motion: centripetal force mv\xB2/r and the time period.","Torque and potential energy, U = \u2212m\xB7B as an extension of the idea.","Simple harmonic motion and T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/\u03BA).","Electric current, I = q/t, and the electron-volt for K = qV.","Resistors in parallel and in series (for the galvanometer conversions)."],path:[{t:"Step 1 \xB7 Fields of currents (Day 1)",d:"Learn the Biot\u2013Savart law and the fields of wires, loops, arcs and the axis of a loop.",ready:"you can find B at the centre of a shape with two arcs and straight leads without a hint.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 2 \xB7 Ampere and solenoid (Day 2)",d:"Learn Ampere\u2019s law, the cylinder, the solenoid and the toroid.",ready:"you can write B inside and outside a thick wire and in a solenoid in under a minute.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 3 \xB7 Forces (Day 2)",d:"Learn the force on a charge, a wire and between parallel wires, with directions.",ready:"you can find the force on a bent wire using its chord.",go:"notes"},{t:"Step 4 \xB7 Motion of charges (Day 3)",d:"Learn r, T, the helix, the strip, the selector and the cyclotron.",ready:"you can find r for a proton and an \u03B1 particle with the same K, the same V and the same speed.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 5 \xB7 Loops, magnets and the earth (Day 4\u20135)",d:"Learn \u03C4, U, the galvanometer, the dipole field, the oscillation and the earth\u2019s elements.",ready:"you can find the shunt for an ammeter, the period of a magnet and the apparent dip.",go:"asked"},{t:"Step 6 \xB7 Practise, then test",d:"Practise by question type, then take the mock test.",ready:"you score 8 out of 10 in practice twice in a row.",go:"practice"}],think:["Which topic is it: the field of a current, a force, a motion in a field, a loop or magnet, the earth or a material?","Draw the directions with the right-hand rule before putting numbers.","Is the charge moving across B, along B, or at an angle?","What is common to the particles: v, K, V or p?","For a wire shape, can symmetry or a line through the point remove some parts?","For a magnet, is the point axial or equatorial, and is r from the centre?","Check the units: cm to m, turns per cm to per m, gauss to tesla."],watch:["The magnetic force does no work; the speed and K of a charge stay the same.","At the end of a solenoid B is half, not equal, to the middle value.","T = 2\u03C0m/qB does not depend on the speed or radius.","Axial B is twice the equatorial B for the same r, and the directions are opposite.","A bent wire in a uniform field: use the chord, not the length of the wire.","Voltage sensitivity of a galvanometer does not change when N is raised (R rises too).","A shunt is in parallel and small; a multiplier is in series and large.","Cutting a bar magnet across its length halves m; the pole strength is the same."],myths:[["Myth: A magnetic field can speed up a charged particle.","Truth: It changes only the direction. The force is perpendicular to v, so the work is zero."],["Myth: A charge at rest in a magnetic field feels a force.","Truth: F = qvB sin\u03B8 is zero at v = 0 and for v along B."],["Myth: A solenoid has the same field everywhere around it.","Truth: Inside it is uniform \u03BC\u2080nI, outside it is nearly zero, and at the end it is half."],["Myth: A bigger radius means a longer time period for a particle in a field.","Truth: T = 2\u03C0m/qB is independent of r and v. A faster particle simply goes on a larger circle."],["Myth: Parallel currents repel because like charges repel.","Truth: Parallel currents attract. The force is magnetic, not electric."],["Myth: A magnet can be split into a north pole and a south pole.","Truth: There are no monopoles. Each piece has both poles (\u222EB\xB7dA = 0)."],["Myth: The field of a bar magnet on the equatorial line is along its moment.","Truth: It is opposite to m, and half the axial value at the same distance."],["Myth: The earth\u2019s magnetic north pole is a north pole of a magnet.","Truth: It is a south pole; that is why the north end of a compass points to it."],["Myth: Diamagnetism occurs only in some special materials.","Truth: All materials have it, but it is very weak and is seen only when there is no para- or ferromagnetism."],["Myth: The same kind of material is best for a permanent magnet and for a transformer core.","Truth: A magnet needs high retentivity and coercivity (a wide loop). A core needs a thin loop, so that the energy loss per cycle is small."]]},extra:[]}})()})();
